-- ownership: idiom I1, an accumulator in a loop (thesis §5.2). The
-- tail-recursive `[ …acc, n ]` extends its accumulator parameter: accepted
-- with `param`, and since slice 2 the caller's side is checked at each call
-- (`ownership-checker.md` §16.4 item 3): the recursive call passes the
-- construction, `countdown` a fresh `[]`, both accepted, so the whole loop
-- would be in place. The fold's callback writes its own accumulator
-- parameter: `callback-param`, decided by `foldl`'s inferred summary, which
-- supplies it owned (thesis I2, §16.4 item 4); the initial `[]` is the
-- caller-side demand. `doubled` builds into a local list it then owns:
-- `fresh`.
pub upTo : Int, List Int → List Int
upTo n acc =
    if n == 0 then
        acc
    else
        upTo (n - 1) [ …acc, n ]


pub countdown : Int → List Int
countdown n = upTo n []


pub squares : List Int → List Int
squares xs = List.foldl xs [] λx acc → [ …acc, x * x ]


pub doubled : Int → List Int
doubled x =
    start = [ x ]
    [ …start, x ]
